    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_4edd3c58f39bb2022b7bc8b496ca07e85b95fcec20cec1d90b22e5711cd32452",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Logarithmic coordinates reduce the rank-two xp problem to a finite overlap system whose Jost determinant distinguishes surviving endpoint channels from their simultaneous cancellation: nonzero F gives scattering, whereas a real zero can yield a normalizable state embedded in the continuum. Ordered-integral analyticity constrains the determinant, and, under the appendix's special phase-factor and decay assumptions, a dispersion relation turns the derivative of F at a zero into the bound-state norm and local density weight.",
